# CLAUDE.md

This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.

## Commands

```bash
# Development
yarn dev                    # Start dev server on http://localhost:3000

# Database
yarn db:migrate             # Run migrations
yarn db:migrate:down        # Rollback last migration
yarn db:migrate:make <name> # Create new migration
yarn db:seed                # Run all seeds (server/db/seeds)
yarn db:seed:make <name>    # Create new seed file
yarn db:codegen             # Generate TypeScript types from database

# Testing
yarn test                   # Run backend tests in watch mode (server/**/*.test.ts)
yarn test:run               # Run backend tests once
yarn test:frontend          # Run frontend tests in watch mode (app/**/*.test.ts)
yarn test:frontend:run      # Run frontend tests once
yarn typecheck              # Run TypeScript type checking
yarn lint                   # Run oxlint (linter)
yarn lint:fix               # Run oxlint with auto-fix
yarn fmt                    # Format code with oxfmt
yarn fmt:check              # Check formatting without writing

# Build
yarn build                  # Production build
```

**Environment:** Set `NUXT_DATABASE_URL` for PostgreSQL connection (auto-binds to `runtimeConfig.databaseUrl`).

## Architecture

### Nuxt 4 Structure
- `app/` - Frontend (pages, components, assets)
- `server/` - Backend (API handlers, database, test utilities)
- `layers/gallop-ui/` - gallop-ui, Gallop's shared UI layer (a git subtree; see below)

**Server imports:** use the `#server` alias for server-to-server imports
(`import { PREVIEW_ANCHOR_USER } from "#server/utils/preview"`), not the app-root
`~~/server/...` path. `#server` points at `server/` and is provided by Nuxt (≥4.3);
backend vitest maps it in `vitest.config.ts`. Use `#server/test-utils` for the
test-utils barrel too — the app-root `~` alias resolves to `app/` under the
server tsconfig project, so `~/server/...` fails `yarn typecheck` on server code.

### Shared UI (gallop-ui)
`layers/gallop-ui` is gallop-ui, the UI layer every Gallop app shares. It is a Nuxt layer whose
components (e.g. `<SegmentedControl>`) register automatically, and it replaces per-app copies of
components and composites. **Before building any UI**, read
`layers/gallop-ui/CATALOGUE.md` and `layers/gallop-ui/CLAUDE.md`, and work down this list:
use a layer component as it is (restyled with `pt` if needed) → extend it in the layer → compose
layer components → build something new (in the layer if another app could use it).

- **Look per app:** colours come from this app's tokens (`layers/gallop-ui/TOKENS.md`); app-wide
  classes go in `app/app.config.ts` under `gallopUi.pt.<Component>`; one-off classes in the `pt` prop.
- **One UI font, set once:** the app's typeface is `--font-sans` in `@theme` in `app/assets/css/main.css`
  (Tailwind puts it on `<html>`, so every page, layout, overlay and layer component gets it). Never set the
  font from a layout (`bodyAttrs` class, a wrapper `font-*`) or on `html`/`body` by hand. A face for one part of
  the app (a marketing site's headlines, a brand wordmark) is a `--font-<name>` token used as a class on those
  elements.
- **Never shadow:** no `app/components` file may register under a layer component's name.
  Adopting a layer component means deleting the app's own copy in the same change.
- **Changing the layer:** edit files in `layers/gallop-ui` in a commit of their own, with no app files
  and a client-neutral message (the layer goes to every client). Check the look at `/_ui` in `yarn dev`
  (every component's stories in this app's theme, development only), then run `yarn ui:impact`: it shows what
  the change does to every app on this machine (call sites, type errors, before/after screenshots). Send it back
  with `yarn ui:push <branch>` and open a PR in gallop-systems/gallop-ui.
- **Updating:** `yarn ui:pull` (latest release) or `yarn ui:pull vX.Y.Z`. Read the layer's
  CHANGELOG.md first. `yarn ui:version` shows the current one.
- If `layers/gallop-ui` is missing, add it with `yarn ui:add`.
- **Checking UI work:** with the app running on throwaway data, `yarn ui:probe --url http://localhost:<port>` loads every
  page at 1280 and 390, light and dark, presses its tables, menus, tabs, dialogs and forms (it blocks every write), and
  reports what a screenshot misses: neighbouring fields that differ, clipped focus rings, menus that don't close on a
  second press, colours that flash mid-transition (`layers/gallop-ui/docs/probe.md`). Sign in with `--login-url` or
  `--storage-state`.

- **Formatting dates, times, money and numbers:** use the layer's auto-imported `formatDate`, `formatDateTime`,
  `formatRelative`, `formatCurrency`, `formatNumber` and the rest (`layers/gallop-ui/FORMATTERS.md`), not
  `Intl`, `toLocaleString` or date-fns `format()` in a component. They work in server code too. Times show in
  `gallopUi.timeZone` (`app/app.config.ts`, set from the time zone chosen when the app was created: `{{ time_zone }}`), so the
  server and the browser print the same text; a `"2025-01-28"` day is never shifted. Pass `timeZone` when it differs per user.

The lefthook pre-commit hook enforces the shadowing and separate-commit rules (`yarn ui:check`); pre-push runs the layer's tests (`yarn ui:test`).

### UI Components (gallop-ui)
Every component is gallop-ui's (`<Button>`, `<Card>`, `<Input>`, `<Select>`, `<Modal>`, `<DataTable>`, ...); there is
no other component library, so look in `layers/gallop-ui/CATALOGUE.md` before reaching for anything else.

**Customization:** `class` is shorthand for `pt.root`; other sections go through `pt`:
```vue
<Button class="rounded-full" />
<Card title="Details" :pt="{ header: 'border-b' }" />
```

### Database (Kysely + PostgreSQL)
- `server/utils/db.ts` - Database connection, exports `db` and `useDatabase()`
- `server/db/migrations/` - Kysely migrations
- `server/db/factories.ts` - Row builders shared by tests AND seeds (`DbLike` type)
- `server/db/seeds/` - Seed files (run via `yarn db:seed`)
- `server/db/db.d.ts` - Auto-generated types (via `yarn db:codegen`)
- `kysely.config.ts` - Migration + seed configuration

**Schema documentation lives in the DB, not just in migration source.** Use
Postgres `COMMENT ON TABLE/COLUMN/INDEX/CONSTRAINT` so the design notes survive on
a live database and flow into the generated types. `yarn db:codegen` runs
`kysely-codegen` (which emits column comments as JSDoc) then
`scripts/inject-table-comments.mjs`, which injects each table's comment plus
`Indexes:` / `Constraints:` sections above its `export interface` in
`server/db/db.d.ts`. Read those JSDoc blocks to understand a table before querying
or changing it, and update the comments in the same migration that changes how
something works.

### Seeds (Convention)
Seeds populate a preview/dev DB with the minimum needed to log in and work.

- **Shared factories**: seeds and tests import the same row builders from
  `server/db/factories.ts`. Tests pass a rolled-back `Transaction`; seeds pass a
  live `Kysely` connection. Uniqueness rides a monotonic `nextSeq()` counter.
- **Order-independent + idempotent by contract**: `kysely seed:run` runs every
  file in `readdir` order with no ledger, so a seed must not depend on another
  having run, and re-running must not throw or duplicate. Rows that must be
  *stable and addressable* (the anchor login user) upsert on a natural key.
- **Validity harness**: `server/db/seeds.harness.test.ts` runs every seed against
  the latest migration and twice-over, catching constraint drift and
  non-idempotency that typecheck can't see.

### Preview / Dev Login
A preview box serves on a dynamic URL that can't be a pre-registered OAuth redirect URI,
so it logs in via a dev-only backdoor instead:

- `server/utils/preview.ts` - `PREVIEW_ANCHOR_USER` (single source of truth, also
  seeded by `server/db/seeds`). Edit it to rebrand the anchor user.
- `server/api/auth/preview-login.get.ts` - establishes a session as that user.
  Double-gated on `import.meta.dev` (compile-time, dead in prod builds) **and**
  `NUXT_PREVIEW_MODE` (runtime). The `/login` button only appears when the
  matching `public.previewMode` runtime flag is on.

### Backend Testing (Transaction Rollback Pattern)
Tests use real PostgreSQL with automatic transaction rollback for isolation.

```typescript
import { test, expect, mockGet, mockPost, expectHttpError } from "#server/test-utils";
import handler from "./index.get";

test("returns users", async ({ factories }) => {
  await factories.user({ email: "alice@example.com", first_name: "Alice", last_name: "Smith" });
  const event = mockGet();
  const result = await handler(event);
  expect(result).toHaveLength(1);
});
```

- `factories` fixture creates test data within transaction
- Always destructure `factories` even if unused (sets up transaction)
- Mock helpers: `mockGet()`, `mockPost()`, `mockPut()`, `mockDelete()`
- `expectHttpError(fn, statusCode)` - Assert handler throws HTTP error

### API Handler Pattern
```typescript
import { z } from "zod";

export default defineEventHandler(async (event) => {
  const db = useDatabase();
  const body = await readBody(event);
  // ... validation with zod, database operations
});
```

## Design System

See `DESIGN_LANGUAGE.md` for the complete design system. Key points:

- **Zinc-first palette** - Use zinc for almost everything
- **Semantic colors only** - Emerald for success, red for danger
- **No decorative shadows** - Only on hover for interactivity
- **Icons** - the app's set, `gallopUi.iconSet` in `app/app.config.ts`: Phosphor (`<Icon name="ph:*" />`) in a new app, Lucide (`lucide:*`) in an app that set `"lucide"`; never mix them. `simple-icons:*` for brand marks. Bundled offline via `@nuxt/icon`.
- **Motion** - `v-auto-animate` (registered globally) for layout changes only; no decorative animation

## Agent Skills

This project depends on `@gallopsystems/agent-skills`, which symlinks Claude Code
skills into `.claude/skills/` on every `yarn install`. Check there before solving
a problem from scratch — the skills that matter here:

- `kysely-postgres` - query patterns, migrations, type generation
- `nuxt-nitro-api` - API handlers, validation, auth, SSR
- `nitro-testing` - the transaction-rollback test harness used in this repo
- `git-github` - branch/PR workflow, debugging failed Actions runs
- `doctl` - DigitalOcean deployments and logs
- `copier-template` - applying template updates (see below)

If `.claude/skills/` is missing, run `yarn install`. To pick up newer skills:
`yarn up @gallopsystems/agent-skills`.

**Contribute lessons back:** if you work through an error, gotcha, or workflow
knot that no installed skill covers (or one contradicts), offer to upstream it:
"Want me to contribute this back to the skills repo?" If yes, run
`/contribute-skill`.

## Template Updates

This project was scaffolded from [gallop-systems/nuxt-copier-template](https://github.com/gallop-systems/nuxt-copier-template). The version it's on is recorded in `.copier-answers.yml` (`_commit`) — never edit that file by hand.

To apply an update, follow the `copier-template` skill (`applying-updates.md` has the full conflict-resolution procedure). The short version:

```bash
git checkout chore/template-update && git pull
uvx copier update --trust --defaults

# resolve conflict markers and *.rej files, then validate
yarn install && yarn typecheck && yarn lint && yarn fmt:check && yarn test:run

git add -A   # must include .copier-answers.yml
git commit -m "chore: update to template vX.Y.Z"
git push
```
